🔷 What is a Class in Java?
A class is a blueprint used to create objects.
It contains:

Data (fields/variables)

Behavior (methods)

✅ Example:
java
Copy
Edit
class Car {
    String color;

    void drive() {
        System.out.println("Car is driving");
    }
}
🔷 What is an Interface in Java?
An interface defines a contract (what a class must do, but not how).
It only contains method signatures (not actual code), unless using default or static methods.

✅ Example:
java
Copy
Edit
interface Vehicle {
    void drive();
}
🔍 Key Differences: Class vs Interface
| Feature                   | **Class**                         | **Interface**                            |
| ------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |
| What it is                | Blueprint to create objects       | Contract that defines behavior           |
| Contains                  | Variables + methods (with code)   | Only method signatures (no code)         |
| Can you create an object? | ✅ Yes (via `new`)                 | ❌ No                                     |
| Inheritance               | Single inheritance                | Multiple interfaces can be implemented   |
| Access modifiers          | public, private, protected        | Methods are `public abstract` by default |
| Implements or extends?    | A class **extends** another class | A class **implements** an interface      |


🧠 In One Line:
A class defines what an object is and does.

An interface defines what an object should be able to do.